{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 41, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Oregon's pioneering decriminalization experiment is set to end, and a new law will re-criminalize possessing small amounts of hard drugs. The new law gives individuals the option to choose between being charged with possession or undergoing treatment.\n\n*** \nThe treatment includes completing a behavioral health program and participating in a deflection program to avoid fines. Those found with personal-use amounts of illicit substances could face up to six months in jail. The law aims to enable law enforcement to tackle public drug use more effectively.\n\n*** \nSupporters argue that the law allows counties to develop tailored programs based on their resources. However, critics warn that this may lead to inconsistent policies across different regions. The law encourages, but does not require, counties to create treatment alternatives to divert individuals towards addiction and mental health services.\n\n*** \nAs people navigate this new law, it's worth considering the broader implications for the country. At a certain age, individuals may be more likely to eat into their savings for shopping sprees, but the new law may put a tongue-lashing on such behavior. P.P.S As the law takes effect, the country will be watching to see how it unfolds, and whether it will have a lasting impact on the way people live their lives, from the food they eat to their shopping habits, and even the way they speak their tongue.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -9.179073458653875e-06}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.4740769863128662}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.0016543286619707942}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -1.0129450559616089}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "ization", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " experim